# Codex review of 332, item 4: the data of the Structure fn creates is copied from the written data through one map in
# which the written data is the new data, keeping the nesting of the copied Structures: Code is under the copy of Box, so
# its kk is Box's own (9) -- the caller's frames hold no kk, and a Code flattened under data would find none. The written
# data of the declaration stays as written. The body over that data is given by the structural link: Code is written
# inside Box and launched by the path `Box\Code()` in the body of probe, over parent(Code) = the copy of Box (step 21.7
# K2, S3: the former tail record `call: data\Box\Code data\Box\Code` still has the two positionals of [args, code]
# and answers 5 here). probe returns the r of that copy: 9.
# Norm: the book #structural-call («The selected Structure C is executed in CODE; its actual parent supplies DATA»).
Observed:
    int: seen 0
end: Observed
fn: probe () int (Box(int: kk 9; Code(int: r 0; data\r := kk)))
    Box\Code()
    return: data\Box\Code\r
end: probe
Main:
    Observed\seen := probe()
